      "document_id": "doc_owasp_top10_a07_identification_and_authentication_failures",
      "version_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32",
      "chunk_idx": 3,
      "chunk_type": "heading",
      "text_content": "## Overview",
      "token_count": 3,
      "chunk_strategy": "structure_aware",
      "heading_path": [
        "A07:2021 – Identification and Authentication Failures",
        "Overview"
      ]
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32_c00004",
      "document_id": "doc_owasp_top10_a07_identification_and_authentication_failures",
      "version_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32",
      "chunk_idx": 4,
      "chunk_type": "paragraph",
      "text_content": "Previously known as *Broken Authentication*, this category slid down\nfrom the second position and now includes Common Weakness\nEnumerations (CWEs) related to identification\nfailures.\n\nNotable CWEs included are *CWE-297: Improper Validation of\nCertificate with Host Mismatch*, *CWE-287: Improper Authentication*, and\n*CWE-384: Session Fixation*.",
      "token_count": 67,
      "chunk_strategy": "structure_aware",
      "heading_path": [
        "A07:2021 – Identification and Authentication Failures",
        "Overview"
      ]
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32_c00005",
      "document_id": "doc_owasp_top10_a07_identification_and_authentication_failures",
      "version_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32",
      "chunk_idx": 5,
      "chunk_type": "heading",
      "text_content": "## Description",
      "token_count": 3,
      "chunk_strategy": "structure_aware",
      "heading_path": [
        "A07:2021 – Identification and Authentication Failures",
        "Description"
      ]
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32_c00006",
      "document_id": "doc_owasp_top10_a07_identification_and_authentication_failures",
      "version_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32",
      "chunk_idx": 6,
      "chunk_type": "mixed",
      "text_content": "Confirmation of the user's identity, authentication, and session\nmanagement is critical to protect against authentication-related\nattacks.\n\nThere may be authentication weaknesses if the application:\n\n- Permits automated attacks such as credential stuffing, where the\n    attacker has a list of valid usernames and passwords.\n\n- Permits brute force or other automated attacks.\n\n- Permits default, weak, or well-known passwords, such as \"Password1\"\n    or \"admin/admin\".\n\n- Uses weak or ineffective credential recovery and forgot-password\n    processes, such as \"knowledge-based answers,\" which cannot be made\n    safe.\n\n- Uses plain text, encrypted, or weakly hashed passwords data stores (see\n    [A02:2021-Cryptographic Failures](A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures.md)).\n\n- Has missing or ineffective multi-factor authentication.\n\n- Exposes session identifier in the URL.\n\n- Reuse session identifier after successful login.\n\n- Does not correctly invalidate Session IDs.\n\nUser sessions or\n    authentication tokens (mainly single sign-on (SSO) tokens) aren't\n    properly invalidated during logout or a period of inactivity.",
      "token_count": 209,
      "chunk_strategy": "structure_aware",
      "heading_path": [
        "A07:2021 – Identification and Authentication Failures",
        "Description"
      ]
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32_c00007",
      "document_id": "doc_owasp_top10_a07_identification_and_authentication_failures",
      "version_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32",
      "chunk_idx": 7,
      "chunk_type": "heading",
      "text_content": "## How to Prevent",
      "token_count": 5,
      "chunk_strategy": "structure_aware",
      "heading_path": [
        "A07:2021 – Identification and Authentication Failures",
        "How to Prevent"
      ]
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32_c00008",
      "document_id": "doc_owasp_top10_a07_identification_and_authentication_failures",
      "version_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32",
      "chunk_idx": 8,
      "chunk_type": "list",
      "text_content": "- Where possible, implement multi-factor authentication to prevent\n    automated credential stuffing, brute force, and stolen credential\n    reuse attacks.\n\n- Do not ship or deploy with any default credentials, particularly for\n    admin users.\n\n- Implement weak password checks, such as testing new or changed\n    passwords against the top 10,000 worst passwords list.\n\n- Align password length, complexity, and rotation policies with\n    National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST)\n    800-63b's guidelines in section 5.1.1 for Memorized Secrets or other\n    modern, evidence-based password policies.\n\n- Ensure registration, credential recovery, and API pathways are\n    hardened against account enumeration attacks by using the same\n    messages for all outcomes.\n\n- Limit or increasingly delay failed login attempts, but be careful not to create a denial of service scenario.\n\nLog all failures\n    and alert administrators when credential stuffing, brute force, or\n    other attacks are detected.\n\n- Use a server-side, secure, built-in session manager that generates a\n    new random session ID with high entropy after login.\n\nSession identifier\n    should not be in the URL, be securely stored, and invalidated after\n    logout, idle, and absolute timeouts.",
      "token_count": 225,
      "chunk_strategy": "structure_aware",
      "heading_path": [
        "A07:2021 – Identification and Authentication Failures",
        "How to Prevent"
      ]
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32_c00009",
      "document_id": "doc_owasp_top10_a07_identification_and_authentication_failures",
      "version_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32",
      "chunk_idx": 9,
      "chunk_type": "heading",
      "text_content": "## Example Attack Scenarios",
      "token_count": 5,
      "chunk_strategy": "structure_aware",
      "heading_path": [
        "A07:2021 – Identification and Authentication Failures",
        "Example Attack Scenarios"
      ]
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32_c00010",
      "document_id": "doc_owasp_top10_a07_identification_and_authentication_failures",
      "version_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32",
      "chunk_idx": 10,
      "chunk_type": "paragraph",
      "text_content": "**Scenario #1:** Credential stuffing, the use of lists of known\npasswords, is a common attack.\n\nSuppose an application does not implement\nautomated threat or credential stuffing protection.\n\nIn that case, the\napplication can be used as a password oracle to determine if the\ncredentials are valid.\n\n**Scenario #2:** Most authentication attacks occur due to the continued\nuse of passwords as a sole factor.\n\nOnce considered best practices,\npassword rotation and complexity requirements encourage users to use\nand reuse weak passwords.\n\nOrganizations are recommended to stop these\npractices per NIST 800-63 and use multi-factor authentication.\n\n**Scenario #3:** Application session timeouts aren't set correctly.\n\nA\nuser uses a public computer to access an application.\n\nInstead of\nselecting \"logout,\" the user simply closes the browser tab and walks\naway.\n\nAn attacker uses the same browser an hour later, and the user is\nstill authenticated.",
      "token_count": 183,
      "chunk_strategy": "structure_aware",
      "heading_path": [
        "A07:2021 – Identification and Authentication Failures",
        "Example Attack Scenarios"
      ]
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32_c00011",
      "document_id": "doc_owasp_top10_a07_identification_and_authentication_failures",
      "version_id": "ver_c4e85c1c4f32",
      "chunk_idx": 11,
